  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Quiet weather through the week with above normal \ndaytime temperatures. Colder temperatures expected early next\nweek.\n\nA broad area of surface high pressure will remain situated over much \nof the eastern US through the work week. The dry airmass overhead, \ncharacterized by PWATs below half of an inch, will support mostly \nsunny skies, and thus ample warming each day. Afternoon high \ntemperatures will climb to above seasonal normals, in the 60s \nareawide today, reaching the upper 60s to near 70 by Friday, 10 \nto 15 degrees above normal. Overnight lows will gradually start \nto warm as low level moisture begins to advect into the region \nlate in the week. Will still see lows in the 20s to 30s this \nmorning, then increasing to the 30s and 40s. \n\nFollowing a frontal passage this weekend, cold high pressure returns \nto the area, and will bring a drop in temperatures for early next \nweek. High temperatures in the 60s are expected for Sunday, but then \nfall into the 40s and 50s by next Tuesday. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Chances for rain this weekend, but amounts look low \nat this time.\n\nThe center of the surface high eventually gets pushed offshore by \nSaturday or so as a cold front approaches the area from the west, \nand a coastal low pressure system tracks along the Atlantic \nseaboard. The high gets wedged down the eastern side of the \nAppalachians, between the low coming from the south and front coming \nfrom the west. This will result in some northeast to easterly flow \nand an increase in cloud cover, so slightly cooler temperatures \nSaturday. Long range models still have some differences in the exact \ntrack this low will take, which will impact how much, if any, rain \nthe area will receive. At this time, looks like any rain will \nbe confined to areas east of the mountains, but plenty of \nuncertainty remains in rainfall amounts. The cold front will \nbring rain chances to the mountains, but, right now, looks like \nrain will fade as the front tracks eastward. Overall, while \nthere are a couple chances for rain this weekend, uncertain in \nhow much rain will actually fall. Behind that front, high \npressure returns to the region, with dry weather for the first \nhalf of the work week.",
